// notify_irq -- NOT an operation. It is the environment's entry point into the
// model: the one thing here the system does not initiate.
//
// Every other function in this tree is called by a PSS action. This one is
// called by the testbench -- a UVM interrupt monitor, a cocotb coroutine, an
// ISR in firmware -- when it observes the DMA's aggregate interrupt output
// assert. NOTHING IN PSS CALLS IT, and that is the point of the design.
//
// WHY NOT A PSS ACTION. The obvious alternative is an interrupt-service action
// looping on an imported blocking wait_irq(). It works, and it costs: the
// action never retires, so every scenario containing an end-to-end operation
// must compose it in `parallel`, and must never wait on it -- the same hazard
// transfer_single.pss documents for auto_restart, imposed on every test in the
// suite. Leaving the notification outside PSS entirely removes it. The channel
// becomes pure runtime plumbing with no scenario semantics, which is the
// condition under which target-time synchronisation is safe to use at all: the
// solver knows nothing about it, and needs to know nothing about it.
//
// TWO CONSEQUENCES OF AN ASYNCHRONOUS FOREIGN CALLER
//
//   1. try_put(), never put(). The caller runs on a simulator thread, outside
//      any executor context. put() blocks when the channel is full, which here
//      would stall the interrupt monitor on a channel nobody is draining.
//      try_put() cannot block, and its cross-executor thread-safety guarantee
//      (§21.9.1) is what makes an asynchronous foreign caller legal at all.
//
//   2. Check your tool emits this. No PSS code references it, so a front end
//      that prunes unreferenced target functions will drop it, and the failure
//      shows up as an unresolved symbol at testbench link time rather than
//      anywhere visible in the model.
//
// WHY IT POSTS TO EVERY CHANNEL, BLIND
//
// A dispatcher that read INT_SRC_A/B to wake only the channels that actually
// raised the interrupt would consume the very state the waiters need: the
// source bits are read-to-clear, and INT_SRC is ANDed with the routing mask,
// so an unrouted channel would never be woken (wait_completion.pss §3.1.1).
// Reading the event in order to route the event is a defect waiting to happen.
// Wake everyone; each thread reads its own CSR and decides for itself.
//
// The cost is bounded by wake's DEPTH of 1: a channel with no waiter keeps at
// most one stale token, worth one extra register read at the top of its next
// operation. That bound is what makes blind posting cheaper than tracking who
// is waiting -- and tracking is not available anyway, since PSS has no mutable
// component attribute to track it in.
//
// try_put() returning false is the NORMAL case, not an error: it means a wake
// is already pending on that channel, which is precisely the coalescing this
// depth was chosen for. The result is discarded on purpose.
//
// If the 31-way post ever shows up as noise, the fix is per-bank channels
// matching INT_A/INT_B -- not waiter tracking.
//
// GATED BY compile if, NOT BY compile assert -- and that is a deliberate
// reversal of what the layering rule would suggest.
//
// The rule elsewhere is that a file whose PRESENCE is a request for blocking
// support asserts rather than vanishes, so the failure is loud. This file
// qualifies: it exists only to feed `wake`, which only the blocking layer has.
// But it is part of THE DEVICE, and `pss.FileSet` selects the device tree
// wholesale from the layout -- so an assert here would not report a mistake, it
// would make the non-blocking profile impossible to build at all.
//
// So the loud failure moves to where the mistake can actually be made: the
// scenario layer, which is separately selected and whose presence really is a
// request for blocking (tests/pss/pss_top.pss).
//
// THIS DOES COST SOMETHING, and it is worth stating. This function already had
// one way to disappear silently -- no PSS code references it, so a front end
// that prunes unreferenced target functions will drop it, and the failure
// surfaces as an unresolved symbol at testbench link time. It now has two. If
// you are staring at an unresolved `notify_irq` from the SystemVerilog side,
// check WHICH PROFILE THE MODEL WAS BUILT WITH before you go looking at
// pruning.
import sync_pkg::*;
import wb_dma_cfg_pkg::*;

extend component wb_dma_c {

    compile if (wb_dma_cfg_pkg::HAS_BLOCKING) {

        target function void notify_irq() {
            bool posted;

            foreach (ch[i]) {
                posted = ch[i].wake.try_put(1);
            }
        }

    }
}
